# Arrows capture this lexically

An arrow function has **no `this` of its own**. It inherits `this` from the enclosing scope where it's written — the one place in JavaScript where `this` behaves lexically instead of dynamically.

```js
const obj = {
  name: 'obj',
  items: [1, 2, 3],
  print() {
    this.items.forEach((i) => console.log(this.name, i));
    // arrow inherits print()'s `this` — no .bind needed
  },
};
```

This is why arrows dominate callbacks inside methods: promise chains, array methods, timers. The pre-arrow workaround was `const self = this` — you'll still read it in old code.

## Where arrows break

**Object methods that need dynamic `this`:**

```js
const counter = {
  n: 0,
  inc: () => { this.n += 1; }, // `this` is the OUTER scope, not counter
};
```

**Constructors:** `new (() => {})` throws — arrows have no `[[Construct]]` and no `prototype`.

**Also missing:** their own `arguments` object (they inherit the outer one, a classic confusion source), and generator arrows don't exist.

## Class-field arrows

```js
class Button {
  handleClick = () => { /* `this` is the instance, always */ };
}
```

Each instance gets its own bound function — convenient for callbacks, but it's per-instance memory instead of one shared prototype method, and it complicates subclassing and mocking. A deliberate tradeoff, not a default.

## The decision rule

If the function needs dynamic `this` (methods), `new`, rebindable `call`, or its own `arguments` — it must not be an arrow. For everything else inside a method, arrows are the right default.

# Callbacks: the receiver doesn't travel

```js
const logger = {
  prefix: '[app]',
  log(msg) { console.log(this.prefix, msg); },
};
setTimeout(logger.log, 1000); // '[app]' is gone — `this` is undefined
```

Passing `logger.log` passes the *function*; the receiver stays behind. Two fixes:

```js
setTimeout(() => logger.log('hi'), 1000); // arrow keeps the call site intact
setTimeout(logger.log.bind(logger), 1000); // or bind the receiver explicitly
```

The arrow wrapper is usually clearer — it preserves the original method call with its dot.

# Event handlers

With `addEventListener`, the DOM sets `this` to the element — but only for non-arrow functions:

```js
button.addEventListener('click', function () {
  this.disabled = true; // `this` is the button
});
button.addEventListener('click', () => {
  this.disabled = true; // `this` is the OUTER scope — bug
});
```

# Classes

Class bodies are implicitly strict, and methods are prototype functions — detachable:

```js
class View {
  constructor() { this.title = 'home'; }
  render() { return this.title; }
}
const v = new View();
const { render } = v;
render(); // TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined
```

Destructuring is method extraction with nicer syntax. Fixes: call through the instance (`v.render()`), bind in the constructor, or use a class-field arrow (see previous lesson's tradeoff note).

# Strict mode changes the default

Sloppy-mode default binding silently hands you the global object — bugs that *work* until they corrupt shared state. Strict mode (all modules, all classes) gives `undefined` instead, failing fast at the property access. When debugging, `TypeError: Cannot read properties of undefined (reading 'x')` right after a refactor that moved or destructured a method call is the telltale signature of a lost `this`.

# The interview drill

For any snippet: (1) find the call site, (2) check for `new`, (3) check for `call`/`apply`/`bind`, (4) check what's left of the dot, (5) otherwise strict-`undefined` or sloppy-global. Five steps, every time, out loud.
